  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nSurface observations show a warm front and 500 mb shortwave draped \nacross northern Indiana. WAA and increased moisture transport have \nallowed for scattered showers and a few storms to persist through \nthe morning and early afternoon, mainly along and south of US 24. \nStill holding onto 20-40% chance PoPs until about 21-22Z this \nafternoon as the warm front will continue lifting northeastward. An \nisolated storm or two will be possible due to surface based \ninstability of around 1000 J/kg this afternoon. Shear and low/mid \nlevel lapse rates will be too weak for any organized strong or \nsevere storms. Northwest Indiana and southwest Lower Michigan will \nlikely remain dry today. There is quite the contrast in sky cover on \nvisible satellite imagery across the area, with clear skies in the \nvicinity of Lake Michigan and overcast skies mainly along and east \nof I-69. Cloud cover is expected to keep highs in the low 80s today \nalong and south of US 24, while temperatures should climb into the \nmid to maybe even upper 80s in locations that have stayed dry so far \ntoday. \n\nThe rest of the weekend appears mainly dry except for 20-30% chances \nfor widely scattered showers and storms Sunday afternoon with a cold \nfrontal passage and associated upper level trough that passes \nthrough the Great Lakes. Rainfall amounts should be light, less than \n0.10\" for most locations. Incoming high pressure behind the front \nwill create a tightening pressure gradient over the area, with \nwind gusts up to 20-25 mph Sunday afternoon and evening. CAA \nassociated with northwest flow behind the front will make for a \npleasant Sunday night with lows in the upper 40s to low 50s. A \nvery fall-like day is ahead Monday with highs only in the low \n70s.\n\nIf you have plans to head to SE Lake Michigan beaches on Sunday, \nstay safe! Behind the cold frontal passage, dangerous swimming \nconditions are expected for southeastern Lake Michigan beaches in \nBerrien and La Porte counties. CAA and northwesterly flow Sunday \nbehind the front will promote onshore flow and waves of 3-5 ft, \nmeaning the swim risk will be High for both La Porte and Berrien \ncounties. Moderate to strong rip and structural currents are \nexpected.\n\nAn upper level ridge will amplify across the central and eastern \nCONUS early next week. Surface high pressure builds over the eastern \nUS on Tuesday, with return flow and moisture from the Gulf ushering \nin warmer than normal temperatures and humid conditions underneath \nthe peak of the upper level ridge over the Great Lakes. Our far \nsouthwest will be the warmest with temperatures in the upper 80s for \nhighs Tuesday and peak heat indices around 95. Another frontal \nsystem likely approaches by midweek with zonal flow aloft as the \nridge breaks down. Have maintained likely PoPs of 40-70% Tuesday \nevening through Wednesday. There are still some uncertainties with \nthis system in regards to exact timing and placement of the cold \nfront. This system may be slow moving or potentially even stall to \nour northwest (still mixed model guidance results on how fast it \nactually comes through our area or not). Rain/storm chances midweek \nwill be highly dependent on where the periphery of the ridge ends up \n(which model guidance has routinely struggled with in recent \nmonths). With forcing expected to be weak as of now, severe weather \nis not expected at this time, but we will have to monitor trends in \nthe coming days. \n\nOur forecast area looks to remain sandwiched between hot and humid \nair to our south and cooler, drier air to our north for the \nforeseeable future, with a trend towards drier than normal \nconditions by late September.",
